! Canonical clause order: S–V–O

A common unmarked clause order is ''subject – verb – object'', with modifiers usually preceding what they modify.

* 我学中文。 — Wǒ xué Zhōngwén. — I study Chinese.
* 他吃饭。 — Tā chī fàn. — He eats / has a meal.
* 师父教弟子剑法。 — Shīfu jiāo dìzǐ jiànfǎ. — The master teaches the disciple sword techniques.

The third example includes two postverbal arguments: recipient/learner and the material taught. Do not assume that every Mandarin verb accepts two objects.

A subject can be omitted when recoverable from discourse context. Topic–comment structure is also important; see [[Grammar/Topic and Comment]].

